sadly bullying can have a long-lasting  effect on mental health and well-being  so today the diana award are raising  awareness about bullying in schools and  online and with the don't face it alone  campaign so we'll be prime minister  please join me in encouraging any young  person experiencing bullying to speak up  and speak out  can i thank him very much and uh  congratulate him on on his  own campaign on this issue i think  everybody understands that bullying is  an absolutely appalling  experience and something that we should  not tolerate in our society and i'm  delighted to see so many colleagues so  many honourable members wearing their  blue ribbons today mr speaker we should  all speak out against bullying but we  should also make sure that we give  everybody the courage and to speak out  against bullying as well  now of course usually the question  doesn't get the chance to respond to the  pm's answer but we're trying to put that  right so today  we can talk to the conservative mp for  watford dean russell who put that  question to the prime minister thank you  for being with us um you i can see  you're sporting your blue ribbon an  enormous amount of mps were doing the  same at pmqs today and what did you make  of what the prime minister said to you  well it's great to have number 10 and  the prime minister's backing of this  fantastic campaign you know the the  campaign itself is in over about two and  a half thousand schools i believe 1.5  million young people are taking part in  uk youth charity the diner awards  activities and in particular around the  don't face it alone campaign so the  opportunity to have the backing of  number 10 they did an event i believe on  monday which featured people like rio  ferdinand talking about their  experiences and their work with young  people but also the opportunity to raise  awareness and i very much appreciate you  having me  on tonight  to tell people that you know don't face  it alone.com if you go there you can get  lots of free resources schools teachers  parents lesson plans videos and really  it's about raising awareness and i was  so fortunate that mr speaker chose me as  a very last question to come in right at  the end  and share awareness of this fantastic  campaign  it's been i've been really struck with  some of the the interviews that i've  done recently and also some of the  stories that have been shared on social  media by some pretty high-profile mps  about their own experiences of bullying  and i know it can be quite a difficult  thing to talk about because it's one of  those things that people tend to kind of  bury quite deeply inside because it's  very painful  you know i spoke to nadeem zaharwe the  education secretary who was talking  about being chased around a park and  dunked under water in the pond by  bullies brandon lewis the northern  ireland secretary uh tweeting today  saying that he was bullied as a teenager  he's dyslexic he was overweight he was  seen as an easy target older kids  thought i was too stupid to succeed too  fat to be taken seriously  you know how important is it do you  think that people in these positions are  open about some of the difficulties that  they face when they were younger  i think it's incredibly important and  it's great to see colleagues be so open  about it i mean i've been campaigning on  mental health and mental well-being for  quite a while now and i was fortunate in  the session to promote the initiative in  watford that i do to train a thousand  middle health first aiders with some  fantastic organizations including the  watford and west heart uh chamber of  commerce who helped deliver that with  volunteers and and the key theme on it  all  is sharing one's experiences learning  from others talking about it and not  being afraid um to share that because i  think the challenge we've seen in  society is there is a stigma and there  has always been a stigma sadly around  mental health and in particular around  bullying and i think actually what this  campaign's trying to do is get people to  talk about it to don't face it alone as  it's called and as the uh as the the  main phrase of it but actually what it's  about is to speak out but also to speak  up and if you're seeing bullying in the  school or your friends and someone's  struggling because of it you know  support them speak up about it and let  people know because  invariably  when i've ever i've spoken to children  about this in young people when they do  make that move actually it totally  removes the bully's power okay um and  actually the power that the bullies  often have is to keep people silenced  and keep them you know so in their  control and actually that's where you  can speak up and and diminish  really important thank you so much  coming on a really important interview  to give thank you
 

